# What `fly deploy` may send to the image builder: exactly what the Dockerfile copies, nothing else.
#
# `fly deploy` uploads the folder it runs in to Fly's builder. On the Mac that folder also holds .env,
# kalshi-private-key.pem and gigabytes of data/, and none of them may leave the machine (CLAUDE.md,
# safety invariants). So this is an allowlist: everything is out unless it is named below, and a new
# secret or data folder stays out without anyone remembering to add it. The GitHub deploy builds from a
# clean checkout and never had the problem; a deploy by hand from ~/Hexagon did.
#
# tools/dockerignore-test.js holds this file to the Dockerfile's COPY lines, both ways: every file a
# COPY takes is let through, and nothing is let through that no COPY takes.
*
!Dockerfile
!package*.json
!src
!tools
!public
!server.js
!README.md
!ops/DEPLOY.md
!ops/entrypoint.sh

# ...and never a key or an env file, even one that turns up inside a folder let through above.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
